Dysmenorrhea
Young Adult & Adolecence
Cyclic lower abdominal cramping, Onset with menses, Radiation to back/thighs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-010
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, incapacitating pelvic pain accompanied by intractable vomiting and inability to retain oral analgesia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage described a sudden onset of agonizing lower abdominal pain with repeated emesis, rendering the patient unable to stand or maintain oral hydration. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critically required to exclude an acute surgical abdomen, specifically ovarian torsion or a ruptured hemorrhagic cyst, and to provide parenteral antiemetics and analgesia to break the pain cycle prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The cramps are so agonizing they shoot down my legs, and I cannot stop throwing up every time a pain wave hits."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the onset of menses 12 hours ago, which was immediately accompanied by recurrent, severe lower abdominal cramping. Over the last 6 hours, the pain has rapidly escalated in intensity, featuring distinct radiation to the lower back and anterior thighs. This severe pain triggered acute nausea and recurrent vomiting during pain peaks, rendering the patient's usual oral NSAIDs completely ineffective. The patient also notes fatigue and concurrent altered bowel habits (diarrhoea). While the cyclic and predictable nature of the discomfort is consistent with previous primary dysmenorrhea, the current severity and intractable emesis represent an acute, unmanageable exacerbation.
Functional Status: Bedbound, writhing in pain, unable to walk to the bathroom independently.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is alone and unable to self-care due to the severity of the acute pain and vomiting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 112/72 | HR: 108 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, diaphoretic, and in acute distress, frequently curling into a fetal position during waves of cramping.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, non-distended abdomen with moderate, generalized suprapubic and lower quadrant tenderness. Crucially, there is no rebound tenderness, involuntary guarding, or palpable pelvic masses to suggest a surgical acute abdomen. Bowel sounds are active. The presentation is entirely consistent with the diagnostic tetrad of cyclic lower abdominal cramping, onset occurring concurrently with menses, and distinct radiation to the back/thighs, confirming a diagnosis of severe primary dysmenorrhea.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin beside the bed; heating pad is present but currently unplugged.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient vomited two doses of over-the-counter Ibuprofen 400mg within 10 minutes of ingestion over the past 4 hours.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Exacerbation of Severe Primary Dysmenorrhea with secondary emesis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ovarian Torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to the clearly bilateral nature of the cramping, the expected cyclic onset directly tied to menses, and the absence of a palpable adnexal mass or unilateral peritonism.

Acute Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, the lack of new vaginal discharge, and the strict cyclic predictability of the pain onset.

Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to a negative point-of-care urine beta-hCG test performed during the visit.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe, incapacitating pelvic cramping and intractable secondary vomiting, entirely precluding the use of standard oral analgesia. The intensity of the pain and the tachycardia raised immediate concern for acute adnexal pathology (such as torsion or cyst rupture), which mandated an urgent after-hours physical evaluation. Clinical examination confidently excluded an acute surgical abdomen and confirmed severe primary dysmenorrhea driven by excessive prostaglandin release. Immediate parenteral intervention was necessary to break the severe pain-emesis cycle, restore comfort, and prevent progressive dehydration, which could not have been delayed until the following morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM stat to provide potent, non-oral prostaglandin inhibition. Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually stat to arrest the intractable vomiting. Recommended scheduling a non-urgent transabdominal pelvic ultrasound via the regular GP to formally exclude secondary structural causes such as endometriosis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To preemptively block the chemical pain cascade, commence NSAID therapy 24 hours prior to the expected onset of menses next cycle, actively preventing the synthesis of prostaglandins rather than trying to manage them once the severe cramping starts. To relax the hyperactive uterine muscle, apply continuous topical heat to the lower abdomen, directly promoting localized vasodilation and muscle relaxation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 weeks to discuss long-term suppressive options, such as continuous oral contraceptives or an intrauterine device.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the pain suddenly shifts to become severe and entirely one-sided, if a high fever develops, or if the vomiting returns and prevents any fluid intake for more than 8 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is primary dysmenorrhea; if symptoms remain refractory to management, secondary causes like endometriosis must be investigated.
Clinical Course: The intramuscular injection will break the current severe pain cycle within 30 minutes, allowing oral medications to be tolerated for the remainder of the menstrual period.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the pain suddenly becomes agonizing on just one side of your pelvis, if you develop a fever, or if you feel faint when trying to stand up.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The severe pain and vomiting are caused by an excessive release of chemicals called prostaglandins, which force your uterus to cramp violently. The injected medication specifically blocks these chemicals without needing to go through your upset stomach.
Actionable Steps: Next cycle, start taking your ibuprofen the day before your bleeding is due to start; this stops the pain chemicals from being built in the first place. Use your heating pad continuously across your lower stomach to help physically relax the cramping muscle.

